    "body": "@annevk \r\n`&nomatch;` - is the ambiguous ampersand and we should report a parse error for it. Let's parse it as per spec:\r\n- We are in the character reference state with a `&` in the `temporary buffer`. Consumed character is `n`, so we reconsume it in the named character reference state.\r\n- Here we need to consume the maximum number of characters matching named character references in the table and attach them to the `temporary buffer`. These characters are `n` and `o` and we don't have a full match.\r\n- Therefore we hit the branch in the named charactere reference state that says: \r\n> If the temporary buffer consists of a U+0026 AMPERSAND character (&) followed by a sequence of one or more ASCII alphanumerics and a U+003B SEMICOLON character (;), then this is a parse error.\r\n\r\nBut temporary buffer contains only `&no`, so we don't report parse error here. And where will **never** be a situation when we have a semicolon in the `temporary buffer` if we don't have a match. So parse error condition is basically unreachable. ",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
